1
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:28,986
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:29,887 --> 00:00:37,895
We are going to continue our discussion on resource optimization, specifically in the context of virtual reality.

3
00:00:37,915 --> 00:00:48,647
So, if we quickly recap what we have done so far, earlier we talked about the basic idea of computer graphics.

4
00:00:49,588 --> 00:00:53,713
Now, why are we talking about computer graphics? Because the

5
00:00:54,470 --> 00:00:59,290
key component of any immersive experience is the rendering part,

6
00:00:59,290 --> 00:01:11,872
how the scene is rendered in front of the eyes of the user on the display device, and the idea of rendering is covered in the discipline of computer graphics.

7
00:01:13,194 --> 00:01:20,443
So, it is essential to know the complexities involved in rendering, and in order to do that, we need to know

8
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:24,493
or at least we should have some idea of computer graphics.

9
00:01:25,194 --> 00:01:27,776
So, we started our discussion on computer graphics.

10
00:01:28,097 --> 00:01:30,579
We have gone through some basic terminologies.

11
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:45,154
Then, after covering the basic idea, we covered the idea of the 3D graphics pipeline and also the stages constituting the pipeline.

12
00:01:45,174 --> 00:01:49,738
We have also covered the first stage of the pipeline, that is, the object representation.

13
00:01:50,005 --> 00:02:05,086
Note that although we are talking about this as the first stage, in object representation, there is no computation as such related to intensity calculation or determination of the pixel intensities.

14
00:02:06,308 --> 00:02:13,077
So, we may or may not call it the first stage, but for the sake of simplicity, we are calling it the first stage of the graphics pipeline.

15
00:02:14,778 --> 00:02:22,685
Now, in this lecture, what we are going to do is cover the second and third stages of the graphics pipeline.

16
00:02:22,786 --> 00:02:25,688
So, two more stages will be covered in this lecture.

17
00:02:26,049 --> 00:02:34,436
But before we do that, let us quickly have a relook at the 3D graphics pipeline as discussed in the previous lecture.

18
00:02:36,859 --> 00:02:41,443
So, we have these five stages: object representation, first stage.

19
00:02:41,693 --> 00:02:45,818
which happens in the local coordinate system.

20
00:02:48,341 --> 00:02:49,843
We have already talked about this one.

21
00:02:51,144 --> 00:03:02,157
The second stage is modeling transformation, in which the local coordinate description is transformed to a world coordinate description; that is,

22
00:03:02,157 --> 00:03:07,624
local to world transformation, local to world coordinate transformation.

23
00:03:08,424 --> 00:03:09,726
Third is the lighting stage.

24
00:03:12,710 --> 00:03:17,038
which takes place in the world coordinate reference frame.

25
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:27,397
Then we have the fourth stage, that is the viewing pipeline, containing several sub-stages starting with viewing transformation,

26
00:03:27,397 --> 00:03:36,692
where again we transform from world coordinate system to a 3D view coordinate system.

27
00:03:38,056 --> 00:03:50,713
This is followed by clipping, an operation taking place in the 3D view coordinate system, followed by hidden surface removal, another operation taking place again in the 3D view coordinate system.

28
00:03:52,295 --> 00:04:03,270
Then we have projection transformation, where we transform from 3D view coordinate to 2D view coordinate system, and then we have window to viewport transformation, where we transfer

29
00:04:03,858 --> 00:04:09,004
the scene description in the 2D view coordinate system to a device coordinate system.

30
00:04:12,048 --> 00:04:25,764
The fifth and final stage is scan conversion, where we transfer or transform from the device coordinate scene description to screen coordinate or pixel grid description of the scene.

31
00:04:27,066 --> 00:04:28,768
So, these are the five stages.

32
00:04:30,368 --> 00:04:39,104
Out of these, we have already covered the first one, and we are going to cover these two stages, namely modeling transformation and

33
00:04:39,104 --> 00:04:44,609
the lighting, or the second and third stages, in this part of the lecture.

34
00:04:46,892 --> 00:04:52,541
So, let us start with stage 2, that is modeling or geometric transformation.

35
00:04:52,581 --> 00:04:58,890
So, both terms are used; either modeling transformation can be used, or we can use the term geometric transformation.

36
00:05:01,317 --> 00:05:03,920
Now, what is the basic idea behind this stage?

37
00:05:05,381 --> 00:05:07,444
Earlier, we talked about object representation.

38
00:05:07,464 --> 00:05:13,150
Now, these object representation techniques allow us to represent objects individually.

39
00:05:13,611 --> 00:05:25,924
So, for each object, we can have an individual definition of the object, and that definition is often called the local or object coordinate system description of the object.

40
00:05:27,524 --> 00:05:42,447
So, essentially, we can think of each object as having its own coordinate reference frame in which the object is defined; that is, its position, dimension, and so on are defined.

41
00:05:42,467 --> 00:05:48,497
Now, in order to construct a scene, what we need to do is put multiple objects together.

42
00:05:48,697 --> 00:05:55,708
Now, each individual object is defined in its own coordinate system, or its local coordinate system.

43
00:05:57,747 --> 00:06:06,275
We need to redefine the object in a common coordinate system where all the objects can be put together and defined.

44
00:06:07,076 --> 00:06:16,425
So, objects need to be assembled together in a different setting, which we can call the scene or world coordinate system.

45
00:06:21,991 --> 00:06:24,193
So, essentially, what we do is basically

46
00:06:24,595 --> 00:06:29,781
taking the object from its local coordinate description to a scene or world coordinate description.

47
00:06:29,881 --> 00:06:32,645
The idea is illustrated here.

48
00:06:34,727 --> 00:06:43,798
So, suppose we have object 1 and object 2, each having its own coordinate system as shown here.

49
00:06:44,439 --> 00:06:52,729
Now, using these two objects, we are constructing a scene which comprises this shape

50
00:06:53,384 --> 00:07:06,575
resembling the English letter H. So, as you can see, in order to construct this H, we have used multiple instances of object 1 and object 2 and that

51
00:07:06,575 --> 00:07:11,064
too in different shapes, sizes, orientations, and so on.

52
00:07:12,266 --> 00:07:18,733
Say, for example, this is one instance of object 1, a second instance of object 1,

53
00:07:27,944 --> 00:07:32,768
a third instance of object 1, a fourth instance of object 1.

54
00:07:33,929 --> 00:07:38,534
So, these are all object 1 and these are all object 2.

55
00:07:43,878 --> 00:07:56,550
So, there are four instances of object 1 and three instances of object 2 used to create this particular shape, and if you compare

56
00:07:58,302 --> 00:08:06,376
the definition to the final position or shape or orientation of the objects, you can see a lot of changes.

57
00:08:07,638 --> 00:08:18,336
So, this is what we are calling transformation from local coordinate definition to world coordinate definition or scene coordinate definition.

58
00:08:21,539 --> 00:08:35,480
So, this modeling transformation, the second stage, implies applying some operations on the object definition in local coordinates to transform to the world or scene coordinate system.

59
00:08:37,102 --> 00:08:41,168
All these operations can be derived from a set of basic operations.

60
00:08:43,813 --> 00:08:46,217
which are the basic transformations.

61
00:08:46,237 --> 00:08:47,619
What are those basic transformations?

62
00:08:47,659 --> 00:09:00,431
We are all familiar with those transformations, such as translation, rotation by some angle either in a clockwise or anticlockwise direction around an axis,

63
00:09:00,431 --> 00:09:11,252
scaling, that is reduction or increase in the size of the object, and shearing, that is changing the shape of the object.

64
00:09:12,914 --> 00:09:13,315
So,

65
00:09:14,864 --> 00:09:27,801
These four constitute the basic transformations, and we can use combinations of these four to create any sort of transformation required to construct a scene.

66
00:09:30,324 --> 00:09:36,552
Now, typically, each transformation is represented in the form of a matrix.

67
00:09:38,405 --> 00:09:46,376
But the matrix representation of transformations happens in a different setting, that is typically called a homogeneous coordinate system.

68
00:09:47,979 --> 00:09:56,070
So, for example, for a 2D transformation, that is a transformation happening in a 2D plane, we can have 3 by 3 matrices.

69
00:09:56,651 --> 00:10:04,422
For a 3D transformation, that is a transformation happening in 3D, we can have 4 by 4 matrices and so on.

70
00:10:04,442 --> 00:10:08,007
So, one extra dimension is added in the homogeneous coordinate system.

71
00:10:10,417 --> 00:10:24,776
So, for example, for translation, we can have this 3 by 3 matrix for 2D translation, where Tx and Ty are the amounts of translation along the x and y directions.

72
00:10:25,397 --> 00:10:31,084
For rotation in 2D, we have this 3 by 3 matrix; phi is the angle of rotation.

73
00:10:32,286 --> 00:10:39,175
For scaling in 2D, we can have this 3 by 3 matrix, where Sx and Sy are scaling factors along

74
00:10:39,712 --> 00:10:40,834
the x and y directions.

75
00:10:43,297 --> 00:10:49,005
For shearing in 2D, the corresponding 3 by 3 matrix is shown here.

76
00:10:49,806 --> 00:10:55,735
Here Sx, Shx and Shy are the shearing factors along the x and y directions.

77
00:10:58,078 --> 00:11:04,347
Similarly, we can have the matrix representation of transformations for 3D operations.

78
00:11:05,669 --> 00:11:05,989
Now,

79
00:11:07,336 --> 00:11:22,896
The matrix representation is a convenient way to represent transformations, and using these matrices we can transform points from an object's own local coordinate system to a world coordinate system.

80
00:11:23,997 --> 00:11:24,678
So, how does it work?

81
00:11:25,759 --> 00:11:33,249
Given a point (x, y), we can assume that it is in the local coordinate system where the object is defined.

82
00:11:34,070 --> 00:11:36,533
We can represent it as a column vector.

83
00:11:39,230 --> 00:11:54,654
Then we can multiply the transformation matrix with the column vector to get the new point in the form of another column vector, P dash,

84
00:11:54,654 --> 00:12:04,815
which we can obtain using the transformation matrix S and the original point P to get the new point.

85
00:12:07,225 --> 00:12:22,302
So, effectively, the transformation refers to a matrix multiplication of the points which are on the surface of the object to the new points that ideally should be in the world coordinate system.

86
00:12:24,244 --> 00:12:33,655
There is one issue, though: sometimes to construct a world coordinate scene, we need to perform multiple geometric or modeling transformations on an object.

87
00:12:33,854 --> 00:12:42,175
That is, not only the basic operations, but multiple operations may be required, and there we need to go for a composite transformation matrix rather than

88
00:12:42,175 --> 00:12:48,850
basic transformation matrices like translation, rotation, scaling, or shearing.

89
00:12:50,972 --> 00:12:59,541
Just to illustrate the idea, or rather the issue, let us consider one example: an object,

90
00:13:00,449 --> 00:13:13,724
which is a rectangle shown here, ABCD, is defined in the local coordinate system which is used to define a chimney of the house in the world coordinate system.

91
00:13:13,785 --> 00:13:22,615
So, this rectangle is used to define a chimney-like shape in this house shown in the world coordinate scene.

92
00:13:23,396 --> 00:13:28,802
So, in order to do that, in order to construct this scene with the chimney in its place,

93
00:13:29,963 --> 00:13:30,804
What do we need to do?

94
00:13:31,125 --> 00:13:47,026
What we need to do is basically transform ABCD, the original rectangle in its local coordinate scene, to A dash, B dash, C dash, D dash, that is this chimney-like shape.

95
00:13:49,189 --> 00:13:53,975
Now, that is not possible with only a single basic transformation.

96
00:13:53,995 --> 00:13:59,683
So, using either translation only, rotation only, scaling only, or shearing only,

97
00:14:00,187 --> 00:14:02,230
we will not be able to do this.

98
00:14:02,471 --> 00:14:08,541
We will not be able to transfer or transform ABCD to A dash, B dash, C dash, and D dash.

99
00:14:10,905 --> 00:14:13,950
We actually require two basic transformations together.

100
00:14:14,752 --> 00:14:17,196
One is scaling and one is translation.

101
00:14:20,642 --> 00:14:24,929
So, in order to perform the transformation, we need to go through stages.

102
00:14:25,310 --> 00:14:26,211
The first stage is

103
00:14:27,102 --> 00:14:32,450
to determine the basic matrices for individual transformations, that is, scaling and translation.

104
00:14:33,892 --> 00:14:43,906
As we can see, from here to this shape in the world coordinate scene, the object is halved in length while the height remains the same.

105
00:14:44,046 --> 00:14:53,259
That means the scaling factor along the x direction must be half, and the scaling factor along the y direction should be 1.

106
00:14:54,541 --> 00:14:56,063
So, the scaling matrix

107
00:14:56,465 --> 00:15:13,774
should be of this form 0.5 0 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 where Sx equals 0.5, the scaling factor along the x direction, and Sy equals 1 since there is no change.

108
00:15:17,941 --> 00:15:19,163
Also, we need to translate.

109
00:15:20,987 --> 00:15:23,643
So, vertex-wise, if we look,

110
00:15:23,643 --> 00:15:35,325
we can see that vertex D(0, 0), that is the origin, has been translated to D dash, that is 55 in the world coordinate scene.

111
00:15:35,825 --> 00:15:48,578
So, the translation matrix should be this one: 1 0 5, 0 1 5, 0 0 1, that is Tx equals 5, Ty equals 5.

112
00:15:51,258 --> 00:15:55,703
Translation along the x-axis should be 5, translation along the y-axis should be 5.

113
00:15:59,388 --> 00:16:02,031
So, now we have identified the two basic matrices.

114
00:16:02,311 --> 00:16:09,600
So, the second step is to obtain the composite matrix, the composite transformation matrix where both are used.

115
00:16:12,564 --> 00:16:19,172
So, the first transformation applied to the object is the rightmost in the sequence.

116
00:16:21,143 --> 00:16:23,547
The next transformation is placed on the left.

117
00:16:24,128 --> 00:16:29,156
This is how we can obtain the composite matrix.

118
00:16:31,459 --> 00:16:34,144
And we continue in this way until the last transformation.

119
00:16:34,164 --> 00:16:37,990
Since we have only two, after the first two steps, we can stop.

120
00:16:38,030 --> 00:16:43,939
That is, the first transformation, whatever it is, we put it on the right-hand side.

121
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:45,582
That is T1.

122
00:16:46,102 --> 00:16:49,686
Then the next transformation is placed on the left of this transformation.

123
00:16:49,706 --> 00:16:52,749
So if the next one is T2, then we put it in this way.

124
00:16:52,769 --> 00:17:01,659
So right-to-left arrangement of transformations that are applied.

125
00:17:06,984 --> 00:17:10,127
And then we perform a matrix multiplication of those transformations.

126
00:17:10,147 --> 00:17:13,712
So if we do the same for our example,

127
00:17:15,194 --> 00:17:21,801
We can obtain the composite matrix by multiplying these two: translation followed by scaling.

128
00:17:22,843 --> 00:17:26,067
So, right to left, then we multiply these two.

129
00:17:30,813 --> 00:17:44,610
So, if we follow the same, then we can see that scaling is the first transformation applied, followed by translation, and we arrange them from right to left as mentioned previously.

130
00:17:46,328 --> 00:18:00,895
And then we take a multiplication, we perform a multiplication of the matrices to get a composite transformation matrix in this form; that is the composite transformation matrix.

131
00:18:03,406 --> 00:18:10,537
In the third step, what we do is obtain the new coordinate positions by multiplying the current vertices with the composite matrix.

132
00:18:10,957 --> 00:18:23,096
Current vertices are given here: A, B, C, D. The composite matrix is this one. Current vertex,

133
00:18:23,116 --> 00:18:32,670
if it is A, then A dash would be given by multiplying M with A. Similarly,

134
00:18:34,962 --> 00:18:48,399
multiplying M with B we get B dashed as shown here; C dashed in a similar fashion we can get, and D dashed similarly we will get like this,

135
00:18:48,399 --> 00:19:02,050
by multiplying M with current vertex D. So, this is how we can obtain the transformed points.

136
00:19:03,700 --> 00:19:05,062
in the world coordinate scene.

137
00:19:07,204 --> 00:19:15,134
One thing we should note here is that matrix multiplication is not commutative, and the sequence is very important.

138
00:19:15,254 --> 00:19:30,973
So, if we are unable to formulate the sequence in the proper order, like right to left as we have discussed previously, then if the sequence is wrong, it may lead to an incorrect result.

139
00:19:32,388 --> 00:19:40,136
So, the order in which we put the transformations before multiplying them is very important.

140
00:19:43,260 --> 00:19:55,273
Nevertheless, without going into further details of all the complexities involved in modeling or geometric transformation, we can say that

141
00:19:55,844 --> 00:20:02,962
modeling transformation involves multiplication of a transformation matrix, whether it is a basic matrix or a composite matrix,

142
00:20:02,962 --> 00:20:12,279
with the vertices to get a transformed vertex in the world coordinate scene.

143
00:20:12,299 --> 00:20:13,480
That is about stage 2.

144
00:20:14,321 --> 00:20:17,524
Let us talk about stage 3, that is, color computation.

145
00:20:21,388 --> 00:20:24,771
So, in the third stage, what we do is basically

146
00:20:25,358 --> 00:20:28,921
we try to assign colors to the objects in the scene.

147
00:20:29,742 --> 00:20:40,533
If a color is not assigned, then we will not be able to perceive depth, which will affect our perception of the 3D scene.

148
00:20:40,973 --> 00:20:45,438
So, essentially, the quality of experience will reduce, as shown in this example.

149
00:20:46,639 --> 00:20:49,061
The top one is without assigning proper color.

150
00:20:50,062 --> 00:20:54,927
Some color is there, but it is not done properly, and the bottom figure shows

151
00:20:55,295 --> 00:21:02,085
If we assign colors properly, then we can get a more realistic perception.

152
00:21:07,093 --> 00:21:11,820
Now, in order to do that, we use lighting or shading models.

153
00:21:14,344 --> 00:21:23,918
The term lighting model and the term shading model are distinctly used in the context of computer graphics, although they may physically represent more or less similar things.

154
00:21:25,248 --> 00:21:30,579
But here the terms are differentiated in terms of the computation involved.

155
00:21:30,599 --> 00:21:36,291
Lighting models are those that involve more computation as compared to shading models.

156
00:21:36,451 --> 00:21:44,708
Shading models are used to reduce computation but without compromising on the quality of experience.

157
00:21:46,949 --> 00:21:49,653
Here, let us focus our attention on lighting models.

158
00:21:49,673 --> 00:21:57,866
Shading models are essentially approximations of color assignment, approximations of methods used to assign colors using the lighting models.

159
00:21:59,528 --> 00:22:01,231
So, they also rely on lighting models.

160
00:22:01,251 --> 00:22:09,784
So, let us try to understand the lighting model that will give a holistic understanding of this color assignment process.

161
00:22:10,591 --> 00:22:21,526
So, lighting models compute colors in terms of continuous intensity values; essentially, these are real numbers typically within the range 0 to 1.

162
00:22:21,887 --> 00:22:25,091
So, typically a normalized real number.

163
00:22:27,194 --> 00:22:35,346
Now, these real numbers are mapped to computer-supported discrete values.

164
00:22:36,968 --> 00:22:40,513
So, we will come back to this point at the end of this lecture.

165
00:22:42,940 --> 00:22:48,490
In order to understand the lighting model, let us first try to quickly understand the idea of color.

166
00:22:50,073 --> 00:22:59,129
Now, a source emits light. Say, for example, this is a scenario: here there is a direct light source; it emits light.

167
00:22:59,149 --> 00:23:03,717
The emitted light energy falls upon a point on the surface, say here.

168
00:23:06,920 --> 00:23:12,628
Sometimes, emitted light falls on the point after getting reflected from surrounding surfaces.

169
00:23:12,688 --> 00:23:21,982
So, this light, after getting reflected from here, comes and falls on the same point.

170
00:23:22,002 --> 00:23:24,024
The incident light then reflects.

171
00:23:24,345 --> 00:23:31,375
So, whatever light is incident at this point gets reflected and comes to our, or the viewer's, eye.

172
00:23:33,937 --> 00:23:38,802
The intensity of light reaching the viewer's eye results in color perception.

173
00:23:38,822 --> 00:23:45,649
Whatever the intensity of this reflected light is determines what color the viewer is going to perceive.

174
00:23:48,291 --> 00:23:53,977
Now, in computer graphics, we are interested in simulating this process through a lighting model.

175
00:23:54,057 --> 00:24:03,927
So, to compute the intensity of this reflected light, essentially the color of the surface point from where the light is coming,

176
00:24:04,750 --> 00:24:08,394
we simulate the process using a lighting model in computer graphics.

177
00:24:11,537 --> 00:24:14,300
Now, there are many such models.

178
00:24:15,021 --> 00:24:18,265
Let us understand the process.

179
00:24:18,325 --> 00:24:22,049
Let us go through one simple lighting model.

180
00:24:23,250 --> 00:24:31,139
In this model, the light intensity reaching the viewer from a surface point can be modeled as the sum of three intensities.

181
00:24:33,061 --> 00:24:34,302
Ambient light intensity.

182
00:24:35,935 --> 00:24:50,853
which you can represent with I amb, then diffuse reflection intensity of direct light I diffuse, I diff and specular reflection intensity of direct light that is I spec.

183
00:24:51,454 --> 00:24:52,655
The idea is illustrated here.

184
00:24:53,456 --> 00:25:02,787
So, whatever light falls on the surface of this object after getting reflected from surrounding surfaces that is called ambient light.

185
00:25:03,138 --> 00:25:14,089
If light is coming directly from the source and getting reflected in a diffuse manner then that is the diffuse light component.

186
00:25:14,109 --> 00:25:28,584
If the light comes from the source and gets reflected with a specular effect that is a shiny bright spot effect then that is the specular reflection component.

187
00:25:29,925 --> 00:25:31,947
So, each type of light

188
00:25:33,901 --> 00:25:42,132
will have its own intensity and these three intensities together after combining these three intensities they come to the viewer's eye.

189
00:25:42,753 --> 00:25:52,147
And correspondingly we can have the simple lighting model which says that the intensity of the incident light on the viewer's eye is the sum of the

190
00:25:52,147 --> 00:26:00,516
incident light due to ambient component, due to diffuse component and due to specular component.

191
00:26:02,302 --> 00:26:09,894
This is the simplistic way of looking at the lighting model.

192
00:26:09,914 --> 00:26:16,223
Now, the reflected light intensity is a fraction of the incident light intensity.

193
00:26:16,243 --> 00:26:20,069
Whatever falls, a fraction of it gets reflected and comes to the viewer's eye.

194
00:26:21,451 --> 00:26:27,921
Now, this fraction is determined by a surface property known as reflection coefficient or reflectivity.

195
00:26:31,900 --> 00:26:43,445
We define three coefficients for the simple lighting model corresponding to three types of incident lights that is diffuse reflection coefficient for ambient light Ka,

196
00:26:43,445 --> 00:26:53,025
diffuse reflection coefficient for direct light Kd and specular reflection coefficient for direct light Ks.

197
00:26:55,648 --> 00:27:01,755
Now, the values of each of these reflectivity

198
00:27:02,377 --> 00:27:19,375
or reflection coefficients vary between 0.0 to 1.0; that is, if the value is close to 0 or 0, then it represents a dull surface with no reflection, as most of the light gets absorbed

199
00:27:19,375 --> 00:27:28,415
by the surface. And if it is close to 1 or 1, that means a shiny, mirror-like surface where all light gets reflected.

200
00:27:28,475 --> 00:27:30,358
So, if we want to generate different effects

201
00:27:31,705 --> 00:27:37,550
between dull and shiny surfaces, we can vary these coefficients accordingly.

202
00:27:40,953 --> 00:27:54,952
Now, to compute the intensity contribution due to the ambient light, what we need to do is simply multiply the ambient light intensity falling on the surface point

203
00:27:54,952 --> 00:28:00,210
with the reflectivity for the ambient light, that is Ka, to get the

204
00:28:02,063 --> 00:28:06,889
fraction of the ambient light that comes to the viewer's eye.

205
00:28:09,251 --> 00:28:11,254
So, this is I_a equal to K_a I_a.

206
00:28:12,775 --> 00:28:16,880
For the diffuse reflection component, it is slightly more complicated.

207
00:28:16,960 --> 00:28:25,370
Here, we need to take into account the surface normal at the surface point from where the light gets reflected and the light source direction.

208
00:28:27,152 --> 00:28:29,995
So, let the surface normal be n, a vector,

209
00:28:31,443 --> 00:28:55,257
and the light source direction be L, another vector. Then we can compute the diffuse reflection component coming to the user's or viewer's eye as Kd Is, where Is is the source light intensity

210
00:28:55,878 --> 00:29:09,078
and the dot product of these two vectors, N dot L, if N dot L is greater than 0; otherwise, it is 0, which means there is no reflection.

211
00:29:14,266 --> 00:29:17,511
Now, specular component calculation is even more complex.

212
00:29:18,993 --> 00:29:22,999
Here we are going to talk about one approximate model.

213
00:29:24,194 --> 00:29:30,800
named after its inventor Phong.

214
00:29:30,820 --> 00:29:37,586
So, the component is I spec equal to KDIS cos NS phi.

215
00:29:37,606 --> 00:29:53,340
Phi is, as you can see in this figure, the angle between the viewer direction and the ideal specular reflection direction; that is, if it is a mirror, then the direction of the reflected light.

216
00:29:55,750 --> 00:30:05,881
Now, we can see from here that cos phi equal to V dot r, another dot product of the two vectors.

217
00:30:07,082 --> 00:30:11,406
V is the viewer direction vector, r is the ideal specular reflection direction vector.

218
00:30:12,628 --> 00:30:22,618
So, we can say I specular is Ks Is, where Is is the source light intensity and Ks is the reflectivity for the specular component.

219
00:30:24,961 --> 00:30:31,250
V dot r Ns if V dot r is greater than 0, otherwise it is 0.

220
00:30:33,312 --> 00:30:45,031
Now, this r, the ideal specular reflection direction, we can represent with the surface normal at that point, the normal vector at that point that is N, and

221
00:30:45,031 --> 00:30:53,820
the light source light direction vector L as r equal to twice L dot N

222
00:30:55,758 --> 00:31:17,923
multiplied by N minus L. So, we can replace R here with this expression to finally come up with the component as a dot product of the vectors L, N, and V. So,

223
00:31:17,923 --> 00:31:23,487
we need to know these vectors in order to be able to compute the specular component.

224
00:31:27,905 --> 00:31:41,011
Now, whatever we have discussed so far assumes that no matter where the light source is, if its emission intensity, if the intensity of the emitted light at the source is IS, then

225
00:31:41,011 --> 00:31:48,836
the same intensity will come to the surface point irrespective of the distance between the source and the surface point.

226
00:31:48,917 --> 00:31:50,779
But in practice, that typically never happens.

227
00:31:54,084 --> 00:31:57,169
Typically, light intensity diminishes.

228
00:31:57,706 --> 00:31:59,208
over distance.

229
00:32:01,011 --> 00:32:07,701
So, we need to take care of this factor that is intensity attenuation over distance.

230
00:32:08,342 --> 00:32:20,120
So, there are two ways to take care of it: one is radial intensity attenuation, that is, the radial factor accounts for the effect of diminishing light intensity over distance.

231
00:32:20,140 --> 00:32:26,329
Typically, an inverse quadratic function is used to compute

232
00:32:27,288 --> 00:32:44,471
this factor in the form f, that is, attenuation factor for radial attenuation, as given by this expression inverse quadratic function where

233
00:32:44,471 --> 00:32:52,854
d is the distance between the source and the surface point; a0, a1, a2 these are coefficients varied to produce a better realistic effect.

234
00:32:52,874 --> 00:32:54,375
So, we can control these values.

235
00:32:56,667 --> 00:33:08,463
Also, angular intensity attenuation can be taken care of, can be considered primarily if we are interested in generating spotlight effects such as shown here.

236
00:33:11,167 --> 00:33:25,847
If we want to have an effect like this, where within a region only those elements will be visible or brightly visible and outside that spotlight zone things will be darker.

237
00:33:26,384 --> 00:33:32,492
If we want to generate such type of effect, then we can make use of intensity attenuation factor.

238
00:33:34,095 --> 00:33:44,549
A commonly used function to determine the factor is this given here: attenuation factor angular.

239
00:33:45,070 --> 00:33:53,742
It is 0 if the surface point is outside the angular limit theta, that is, the range of the spotlight cone.

240
00:33:55,426 --> 00:34:03,447
And if it is within that angular limit, then it varies in the form cos A1 phi where

241
00:34:03,447 --> 00:34:10,592
A1 is the angular attenuation exponent, having a positive value, and we can vary this value to achieve different effects.

242
00:34:13,538 --> 00:34:15,521
So, this is for angular attenuation.

243
00:34:16,042 --> 00:34:21,231
So, if we take into account this attenuation factor, that is, the light source intensity changes

244
00:34:24,029 --> 00:34:29,054
based on the distance of the surface point from the light source.

245
00:34:32,518 --> 00:34:37,225
Then, taking into account this attenuation, we can modify the simple lighting model.

246
00:34:37,284 --> 00:34:40,529
Earlier it was simply IM plus ID plus ISPEC.

247
00:34:41,110 --> 00:34:53,985
Now we can say IM; here we are not taking into account the intensity attenuation, but for diffuse and specular reflection, we are doing that by multiplying these two factors with the sum of

248
00:34:54,657 --> 00:34:55,819
these two components.

249
00:34:56,679 --> 00:35:04,870
Now, if these are one, that means the factor is uniform, meaning we are not taking them into account; then it boils down to the earlier formulation.

250
00:35:06,072 --> 00:35:15,484
If it is not one, then the effect is taken care of.

251
00:35:15,504 --> 00:35:20,010
Also, when we are thinking of assigning colors,

252
00:35:20,351 --> 00:35:22,133
so, the colored light model

253
00:35:23,665 --> 00:35:25,107
is a slightly different way.

254
00:35:26,068 --> 00:35:36,923
Emitted light intensity has three components in such a case, rather than a single component; in a colored light source, these correspond to the primary colors red, green, and blue.

255
00:35:37,564 --> 00:35:44,433
Now, these three intensities combine together to give the perception of any color.

256
00:35:46,817 --> 00:35:52,204
So, the source intensity is a three-element vector Is: Isr, Isg, Isb.

257
00:35:52,775 --> 00:35:54,637
corresponding to the three primary colors.

258
00:35:55,318 --> 00:36:05,279
Reflection coefficients also need to be specified as three-element vectors: k equal to one for red,

259
00:36:05,279 --> 00:36:14,120
one for green, one for blue; kd again one for red, green, blue; and ks one for red, green, and blue.

260
00:36:16,243 --> 00:36:22,410
So, when we compute color, we have to individually compute these

261
00:36:23,825 --> 00:36:31,732
components, and then once these three are generated together, we perceive the color.

262
00:36:32,973 --> 00:36:36,156
Now, so far we have talked about a single light source.

263
00:36:36,176 --> 00:36:39,179
What happens when there are multiple light sources?

264
00:36:40,280 --> 00:36:42,402
Let us assume that there are n light sources.

265
00:36:43,182 --> 00:36:45,945
So, in such a case, we need to further revise the model.

266
00:36:46,065 --> 00:36:51,690
It will be IP equal to I ambient plus summation.

267
00:36:52,090 --> 00:36:53,832
A summation term is introduced,

268
00:36:55,195 --> 00:37:05,825
i from 1 to n, that is, for each light source we have to calculate this part, and then we

269
00:37:05,825 --> 00:37:14,258
have to add those up, and each of these we need to compute for individual colors for the colored light sources.

270
00:37:14,899 --> 00:37:23,329
So, if we have multiple colored light sources, then this needs to be done for individual colors for the colored light sources.

271
00:37:24,676 --> 00:37:29,580
So, you can get some idea of the kind of computations involved in assigning colors.

272
00:37:30,901 --> 00:37:36,166
In the previous case, we have seen the computation involved in transformation.

273
00:37:36,226 --> 00:37:39,309
Now, we are seeing the computation involved in assigning colors.

274
00:37:40,890 --> 00:37:42,892
The other part is intensity mapping.

275
00:37:42,952 --> 00:37:53,181
So, the lighting model computes an intensity value for a pixel, but then that value can be anything between 0 and 1.

276
00:37:53,920 --> 00:37:54,862
An infinite range.

277
00:37:55,523 --> 00:38:02,053
However, devices do not support arbitrary ranges; they have a finite range of values.

278
00:38:02,073 --> 00:38:13,371
Say, for example, an 8-bit display will support 2 to the power 8 or 256 colors for each of the pixels.

279
00:38:13,451 --> 00:38:16,656
So, we have 256 values supported by the device.

280
00:38:19,200 --> 00:38:19,641
But then,

281
00:38:20,566 --> 00:38:23,911
the range 0 to 1 is infinite; any value can be generated.

282
00:38:23,931 --> 00:38:42,416
So, what we need to do is we find the nearest value to IP from the device-supported intensity values, say I k, then the frame buffer stores the bit pattern for I k at the pixel location.

283
00:38:42,436 --> 00:38:50,387
So, how to do that? Essentially, what we need to know, what we need to do, is determine the n number of device-supported levels.

284
00:38:53,152 --> 00:39:06,909
Then we need to determine the minimum device-supported intensity value I naught using a photometer, and we know that the maximum value can be 1.

285
00:39:07,350 --> 00:39:12,236
So, we have I naught and 1 known, and n, the number of levels supported.

286
00:39:12,837 --> 00:39:19,665
Then there is a factor r which we need to compute using the equation I n equal to I

287
00:39:19,966 --> 00:39:21,447
r to the power n I naught.

288
00:39:21,648 --> 00:39:27,442
Now, this equation is formed based on one of our psychophysiological properties, that is,

289
00:39:27,442 --> 00:39:35,280
we can perceive relative change between two intensity values rather than absolute change in the intensity values.

290
00:39:38,403 --> 00:39:47,391
Based on that, we can formulate this equation as successive intensity values to be represented by this.

291
00:39:47,962 --> 00:40:10,257
i1 equal to r to the power n i0, r to the power 1 i0, i2 equal to r square i0, i3 equal to r cube i0 and so on up to the nth value.

292
00:40:13,662 --> 00:40:32,222
Now, once R is determined, we need to calculate these values I0, I1, I2, I3, store that in a table, and for each such value we assign a bit pattern and also store that along with those values.

293
00:40:33,424 --> 00:40:40,311
Then we determine the device intensities and bit patterns and store them in a table, and finally,

294
00:40:41,118 --> 00:40:50,307
when we compute using a lighting model pixel intensity, we map the computed intensity to the nearest device intensity.

295
00:40:50,327 --> 00:41:10,888
So, we have a table storing I0, I1, I2, IN and corresponding bit patterns, bit pattern 1, bit pattern 2, bit pattern 3,

296
00:41:13,484 --> 00:41:25,211
n. So, once we have the computed value, we map it to the table; that means we map it to the nearest intensity, and the corresponding bit pattern is obtained and

297
00:41:25,211 --> 00:41:32,046
stored in the frame buffer. That is how we map a computed value to the device-supported value.

298
00:41:32,066 --> 00:41:34,469
So, let us summarize what we have covered so far.

299
00:41:36,131 --> 00:41:39,255
In this part of the lecture, we have covered two more stages

300
00:41:39,927 --> 00:41:43,712
of the pipeline: modeling transformation and intensity calculation lighting.

301
00:41:43,732 --> 00:41:53,300
In modeling transformation, we have seen that to transform a point from its local coordinate system to the world coordinate system, we first need to

302
00:41:53,300 --> 00:41:58,753
obtain the transformation matrix, which can be an individual basic matrix or a composite matrix.

303
00:41:59,233 --> 00:42:07,825
A composite matrix is more common, and then we multiply the point vector with the composite matrix to determine the transformed point. That is

304
00:42:08,665 --> 00:42:10,548
one set of computations.

305
00:42:10,568 --> 00:42:19,171
Once the point is transformed in the world coordinate scene, then for that point, we assign some color; that is, we compute the

306
00:42:19,171 --> 00:42:23,768
intensity of the light which gets reflected from that point and comes to the user's eye.

307
00:42:24,910 --> 00:42:32,201
Note that here we are not actually computing color at the pixel level, but color in the 3D scene at the point level.

308
00:42:33,022 --> 00:42:35,807
And in order to do that, we need a lighting model.

309
00:42:36,508 --> 00:42:36,748
We have

310
00:42:37,893 --> 00:42:49,926
quickly gone through a simple lighting model to get some idea, and we have also talked about how to map the color computed with the lighting model to a color supported by the device.

311
00:42:51,207 --> 00:42:59,636
The mapping table is device-specific. For a given display, the table is fixed, and we need to map it to the supported value.

312
00:43:00,858 --> 00:43:05,082
In the next part of the lecture, we are going to cover the remaining stages of the pipeline.

313
00:43:06,226 --> 00:43:11,534
I hope you could follow the topics and understand the concepts covered here.

314
00:43:12,075 --> 00:43:16,322
For more detail, of course, we are keeping things at a very broad, simple level.

315
00:43:16,702 --> 00:43:22,391
For more details, you can always refer to the material referenced at the end of this lecture.

316
00:43:24,154 --> 00:43:26,618
So, looking forward to meeting you in the next part of the lecture.

317
00:43:27,339 --> 00:43:29,382
Thank you, goodbye, and happy learning.
